        { code: '// EnemyController.cs — inside Update().', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Update()', what: '' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        Vector2 direction = (target.position - transform.position).normalized;', what: 'The arrow from the enemy to the waypoint it is walking toward, at a length of exactly 1: subtract the two positions, then normalize — the pair you used on the diagonal in 1.5 and to aim a projectile in 3.2.' , why: '**Do not skip `.normalized`.** Without it the vector is as long as the distance to the waypoint, so the enemy sprints when it is far away and creeps when it is close — and on a long beat it covers the whole waypoint in a single frame and never arrives. A position is a Vector3 and a velocity is a Vector2; Unity converts between them by dropping `z`, which is harmless in a flat world.' },
        { code: '        body.linearVelocity = direction * moveSpeed;', what: 'Hands the body a velocity instead of moving the Transform yourself — the movement method you chose from the 2.2 table, and the only reason a wall can stop the enemy at all.' , why: '**No `Time.deltaTime` on this line.** Velocity is measured in units *per second*, so the physics engine already applies the time for you. Multiply by `deltaTime` and the enemy\'s speed starts depending on your frame rate.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, target.position) < arriveDistance)', what: 'The arrival test: the distance check from 3.1, with the threshold you declared in step 1 instead of a radius. True on every frame the enemy is standing within `arriveDistance` of the waypoint it was walking toward.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '', blank: true, what: 'The turn. The enemy has arrived at the waypoint it was walking toward — hand the memory from step 2 the OTHER end of the beat. Nothing else about the enemy changes at this moment: not its position, not its speed, not the waypoints. An `if`/`else` you already know is enough to write it.' , hint: 'The field holds which end it is walking toward. Ask it which one it is holding right now — a Transform can be compared with `==` — and send it to the other. Both waypoints are in scope, and only one of them is the right answer on any given frame.' , why: '**Leave this line out and the enemy walks to the first waypoint and stands there forever.** Nothing else in the file ends the journey: on the Player, letting go of the keys was the turn, and the enemy has no keys. **And a line that always writes the same waypoint is only one beat** — it arrives, sends itself back to the place it is already standing, and never walks again. The two ends have to alternate, which means the line has to *ask* what the field is holding.' },
