<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret never moves. This enemy walks a beat with nobody at the keyboard, and it ships as a
    picture with an empty script: no collider, no body, and no memory of anything. Today it gets
    all three.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy follow a patrol route?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('4.1', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> count the <code>MobileEnemy</code> instances in the
            Overworld: <strong>four</strong>, against exactly one Coin. Write the count down before
            you move anything.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code>, and select
            the prefab <em>asset</em>, not an instance in the scene. Its root carries a Transform,
            an empty <code>EnemyController</code> and one child named <em>Sprite</em> — and no
            collider and no body at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → Box Collider 2D</strong>, on the <em>root</em>. Then
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> drag the handles until the green outline covers the enemy
            you can actually see: the art is not on the root, it lives on the <em>Sprite</em> child,
            offset and scaled up, so a default collider on the root is a small square in the middle
            of a much bigger body.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Add Component → Rigidbody 2D</strong>, then set <strong>Body Type =
            Dynamic</strong>, <strong>Gravity Scale = 0</strong> and <strong>Constraints → Freeze
            Rotation Z ☑ ON</strong>. Only a Dynamic body is stopped by the world, and only a frozen
            rotation keeps a shoved enemy from spinning.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play with no patrol code at all, then walk into an
            enemy. It is solid now, and it still does not move on its own. Both halves of that are
            the point of today.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → right-click → Create Empty</strong>, twice, in the Overworld. Rename
            them <strong>WaypointA</strong> and <strong>WaypointB</strong>, and place them with the
            Move tool in the <strong>Scene view</strong> at the two ends of the beat you want — the
            setup for the waypoint row. If you picked a different row in the table below, you need
            no scene objects at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select each enemy instance, then <strong>Inspector
            →</strong> drag <strong>WaypointA</strong> and <strong>WaypointB</strong> into the two
            slots on <code>EnemyController</code>. An empty slot is a null reference: it throws on
            the first frame your code reads it, which looks like a patrol bug and is really a wiring
            bug.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        Read the three rows below before you write anything. All three are the same four parts in
        different clothes, and only what the field remembers differs — a sign, a target, or a limit.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>The row</th><th>What it remembers, and how it turns</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A leash from the spawn point</strong></td>
            <td>The position it started at, plus how far it may wander. It measures the drift with <code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code> and flips the stored sign once that exceeds the leash.</td>
            <td>No scene setup at all, and the beat follows the enemy wherever you drag it. But it is always a straight line centred on the spawn point, and the turn is noticed only <em>after</em> the enemy has moved — so it always overshoots by up to one frame of travel.</td></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Two placed waypoints</strong></td>
            <td>Two Transforms wired into two Inspector slots. It walks toward the current one and hands the field the other when the distance falls under the arrived threshold.</td>
            <td>You must create, place and wire two objects, and invent a threshold small enough to be accurate but large enough to be reachable — travel further in one frame than the threshold and the enemy sails straight past and never turns.</td></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Two absolute bounds</strong></td>
            <td>Two numbers, a minimum and a maximum, and a sign the movement multiplies by.</td>
            <td>The simplest maths and the most brittle. Those bounds are world coordinates, so moving the enemy leaves its beat behind and a copied prefab turns on frame one. Let the sign ever reach <code>0</code> and the walk stops for good.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Designing a Beat</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the three beat definitions fits an enemy you will place four or five times across a map and keep nudging while you design the level? What does your choice cost you?</li>
<li>The leash approach stores the position the enemy started at. Which lifecycle method must save it, and what would the enemy do if you saved it every frame instead?</li>
<li>A single int holding <code>1</code> or <code>-1</code> is the enemy's entire memory of where it is going. Why is that enough — and what extra state would a bool such as <code>isGoingRight</code> force you to keep in sync?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

